# Fixed 2026-10-02, defect 130's class, lane recovery-b6 (panel 183 R3, the
# author's route (b) of 2026-10-01). Symptom: where panel 183's rule ended
# the reach of a bracket left open, at a line whose first word no bracket
# can hold (R1) or at a name after a line that kept its end inside a `(`
# (R2), the opener was told *never closed*, which is false of a bracket the
# author closes below it (the rule's known cost, in
# `panel-183-a-statement-inside-a-bracket-closed-below-is-the-rules-known-cost`).
# Cause: the lexer had one message for every opener it found open, written
# for the file's end. Each opener is now named still open at the line and
# the word where the rule ended its reach, counted over the blank and comment
# lines between; at the file's end, and at a closer that closes what the
# opener stands in, a body's `}` or the `)` of a call around a `[`, it is
# still never closed, since past that closer no closer can be its own.

function g(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64
    return a + b

# Two openers, both named at the one line that ended their reach.
function two_at_one_line(c: bool)
    x = g(g(1,  #~ unclosed_bracket
    if c
        print(1)
    print(x)

# A blank line and a comment between the opener and the line that ended it.
function lines_between(c: bool)
    x = g(1,  #~ unclosed_bracket

    # a note the count passes over
    while c
        print(1)
    print(x)

# A name after a line that kept its end, a blank line between them.
function a_name_below_a_blank()
    x = g(1, 2  #~ unclosed_bracket

    y = 3
    print(x + y)

# A closer that closes what the opener stands in: the `[` is never closed.
function inside_a_call()
    print([2, 3)  #~ unclosed_bracket
    print(4)

# A body in braces: its `}` closes what the `(` stands in.
function in_braces() {  #~ missing_body
    x = g(1  #~ unclosed_bracket
}

function main()
    two_at_one_line(true)

# At the file's end the opener is never closed.
function at_the_end()
    x = g(1  #~ unclosed_bracket

# Moved 2026-10-07 with defect 410's repair (lane b14-parse): the openers
# of one line the lexer named open at one place are one report, at the
# first, saying how many more stand after it on its line, where each was a
# report of its own: `two_at_one_line`'s `g(g(1,`, both still open at the `if`.
